Philippe, Gabor, and others,

The Wiki's growth is amazing. Thank you so much for doing this. I have little doubt that the results of
your labors will greatly alter the way that beginners and experienced users learn about R.

I totally agree that the contents of the Wiki should not and will not be under the administrators control.
However, I do believe that the structure of the Wiki is. The TCL wiki that Gabor referenced is an excellent
example. The designer of the Wiki has put forward a directory that structures the manner in which a
visitor will investigate the contents of the Wiki. 

My humble suggestion was that one way SOME of the contents of the Wiki could be organized is by
type of example. The way that I'll contribute to the Wiki is by contributing functions that I've written for
my own use. In the TCL Wiki, there is a link on the introductory menu "Browse Wiki Categories".
Allowing the user to quickly investigate worked examples.

I see a thoughtful Wiki example as a near perfect example of literate statistical practice. It's possibly
what journals of the future might look like.

Thanks,

Damian 

